  {
    "episode_id": 92376,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92376.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "I don't know that you get over it. I think you just you have to confront it and deal with it head on. I always say the PRC versus, you know, Chinese Americans, for example, to make it clear that when I'm talking about the PRC, I'm talking about the government, the Communist government, the authoritarian government, and they're very bad policies. So I always try to make it clear in my language that I use the right verbiage. You know, I think, unfortunately, part of this rise that we've been hearing is directly linked to our previous president, President Trump, where he started his presidency by really going after Muslims, as if, you know, a religion is a racial group or an ethnicity, but, you know, the ban on people coming to the US and Muslim countries, I was the start of the Trump presidency, and it ended with him blaming East nations in particular for the COVID-19. So this, I don't think it's accidental that you've seen this level of violence and discrimination happen in the last four years. We had a president who really championed it. In contrast to what happened if you look at what happened immediately at the 9-11 when President Bush really stepped up and said this is, you know, we, you know, Muslim Americans are Americans, this is not about Muslim Americans, these attacks, but by terrorists, one group of terrorists. And I thought that was a very responsible thing to do. So we as a nation need to come together and talk about the fact that we have this history of discrimination against, I call it all the different flavors of Asians. One of the things about Asian Americans Native Hawaiian and Pacific Islanders is that we are many flavors. We come in many different groups, but all of them have face discrimination. And we're all treated as an other. We're rarely treated the way Irish Americans and Polish Americans are treated in Chicago, right? And no time that I ever have When I was in the Army, and my unit was all like Irish guys and Polish guys, then we never asked my buddies where they were from, although they asked me that all the time.",
